;; A declared return type that disagrees with the body was unchecked whenever a
;; by-value aggregate was on either side of the mismatch: `tur check` exited 0
;; and the failure surfaced as a `cc` error inside generated C ("incompatible
;; types when returning type 'tur_adt_S' but 'int64_t' was expected") -- no
;; source span, no diagnostic code, and a message naming types the user never
;; wrote.  The same mismatch between two primitives was caught properly, which
;; is what kept the hole invisible.
;;
;; The return-position dispatcher's tolerances are all calibrated to the int64
;; carrier -- a bare integer where a handle is declared is a real bridge because
;; both are `int64_t` in the emitted C.  A by-value record ADT is not: it lowers
;; to a real `tur_adt_S` aggregate, so there is no shared representation to
;; bridge.  See docs/archive/struct-return-type-mismatch-unchecked-until-cc.md.
;;
;; requires.compiled: on the interpreted path every value is a boxed handle, so
;; an ADT under an integer return IS a bridge there and is deliberately allowed.
(defstruct S [a : int])

(defn f [x : S] : int x)

(defn main [] : int 0)
